ARTICLES = {
"coauthor": {"non_row": "coauthor",
 "kernel": "CoAuthor is a research dataset of human-AI collaborative writing by Mina Lee, Percy Liang and Qian Yang (Stanford University and Cornell University), published with a CHI 2022 paper. It holds the interactions between 63 writers and four instances of GPT-3 across 1445 writing sessions in English, each session recorded at the keystroke level so that it can be replayed.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 7],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["Its paper presents it as \"a dataset designed for revealing GPT-3's capabilities in assisting creative and argumentative writing.\" In each session a writer received a prompt and an instance of GPT-3, wrote freely, asked the model for suggestions, accepted or dismissed them, and edited the accepted suggestions or earlier text in any order. The tab key brought suggestions in a text editor whose code is published. The instances are GPT-3's davinci engine at two randomness settings, high (temperature 0.75) and low (temperature 0.3)."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Every interaction was recorded at the keystroke level with timestamps, \"so that we could replay them precisely,\" and the dataset is published with an interface for replaying the sessions. The creative writing holds 830 stories by 58 writers and the argumentative writing 615 essays by 49 writers, written to prompts from the WritingPrompts subreddit and The New York Times. Stories and essays run to 418 words, a session averages 11.8 queries, and 72.3% of suggestions were accepted.",
                     "The writers were \"Qualified crowd workers from Amazon Mechanical Turk.\" The browse page lists each session by type, prompt, randomness, writer and session ID, the writer held by a short identifier such as \"A1198\"."]),
  ("Authorship in the record", ["The dataset divides its text by origin, \"Percentage of text written by humans: 72.6%\", and one of its tutorials is for \"Identifying authors of sentences\". The paper reads the model's part as contribution, revealing it as a writing \"collaborator\" under various definitions of good collaboration. In the paper as its overview reports it, writers felt stronger ownership when they authored a larger proportion of the final text (r=0.3), and the amount of editing showed none. Amazon announced that Mechanical Turk, the platform the writers were recruited from, would close on 30 September 2026."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The authorship census holds CoAuthor as an empirical comparator for a narrower question, what a language model contributes to a writing session, read from recorded interactions. It reads the dataset as process evidence of distributed contribution held at the session level, the persons counted and the question of whose name the text bears left outside the record.",
                          "The archive keeps apart two questions that a share of human-written text joins: whether AI played a material role in the production chain, and whether AI-produced language survived into the final text. On its definition of the pristine fallacy, \"'human-written' describes the author, not the distribution.\" It reads authorship as \"an accountability structure\" and keeps responsibility for a work apart from sole origination.",
                          "Asked \"CoAuthor dataset\" on 1 November 2026, Google's AI Overview composed the dataset from five cards, gave the writers as crowd workers, named none of them, and left out the paper's frame of the model as \"collaborator\"."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 4, 5, 6]], [[7, 8, 9], [10, 11]], [[14, 20, 15, 13]], [[28, 29], [16, 17, 18], [35, 36]]]},

"in2writing": {"non_row": "in2writing",
 "kernel": "In2Writing, the Workshop on Intelligent and Interactive Writing Assistants, is an interdisciplinary workshop series begun in Dublin in May 2022 that alternates its venue between HCI and NLP conferences, and its proceedings volumes gather peer papers on writing assistants, the 2022 and 2025 volumes held in the ACL Anthology. Its fifth workshop is announced for 2 November 2026 at UIST 2026 in Detroit.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 3, 4, 8, 7],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The series gives its purpose as \"to facilitate discussion around writing assistants, thereby enhancing our understanding of their usage in writing process and predicting the consequences.\" It brings together researchers from human-computer interaction and natural language processing by alternating its venue between the two each year. Each Anthology volume names seven editors, and the series' current site gives its organizers one shared address and names none of them. The Association for Computational Linguistics publishes the volumes; materials published from 2016 on are under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence, and the Anthology is built by volunteers."]),
  ("Its editions", ["The First Workshop met in Dublin in May 2022 among the events of the 60th Annual Meeting of the ACL. The second met at CHI 2023, and the third at CHI 2024 in Honolulu, its proceedings published by ACM. The fourth met in Albuquerque in May 2025 with the Nations of the Americas Chapter of the ACL. The fifth, at UIST 2026 in Detroit, is a hands-on, systems-centric workshop on \"designing and building cohesive writing ecosystems\", joined without a paper submission. The ACL Anthology holds the first and fourth volumes only."]),
  ("What the volumes hold", ["The venue page counts \"15 papers\" for 2022 and \"11 papers\" for 2025; each list opens with its front matter as an entry, so the volumes hold fourteen papers and ten. The 2022 papers include a study of machine-in-the-loop story writing, a story-completion paper written with a professional novelist, and the revision system R3 with its interaction dataset. The 2025 papers include writing assistants for scientific figure captions, an Arabic writing assistant, and a survey of 1,005 university students in Tanzania.",
                            "The 2022 volume holds a reader study, The Pure Poet, in which participants decided \"whether a continuation was written with an AI-tool or not\" (Study 1, N=120; Study 2, N=302). The readers \"generally had low accuracy for differentiating between text types but were overconfident in their decisions,\" and rated the AI continuations lower in style than the human-written and original ones."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The series holds research on human-AI co-writing under the researchers' bylines, and the participants, students, novelists and writers its papers study appear only as the papers report them. Within the papers, authorial function under machine participation is a research subject: a 2025 paper on \"Control, Ownership, and Awareness in Human-AI Co-Writing\" argues that interfaces requiring human interaction throughout generation \"can promote cognitive engagement, agency, and thoughtful decision-making\". How the Anthology holds a name variant or pseudonym is not recorded.",
                          "The authorship census reads The Pure Poet as meeting the question of its second anchor, AI Calls You Fake (#1722), \"directly\". The archive's papers set the human/AI binary against the conditions of writing: \"'human-written' describes the author, not the distribution.\""])],
 "ko": [[[1, 3, 9, 10, 11]], [[4, 5, 6, 7, 8]], [[13, 14, 15, 16], [22, 23]], [[17, 18, 30], [24, 26]]]},

"creative-ai-lab-database": {"non_row": "creative-ai-lab-database",
 "kernel": "The Creative AI Tools & Resources Database is an ongoing collection of tools and resources for artists, designers, engineers, curators and researchers who bring machine learning and AI into their practice, produced by the Creative AI Lab, a research partnership of Serpentine and the Department of Digital Humanities at King's College London. Launched on 9 July 2020 and published at creative-ai.org, it lists each tool under its developer and each resource under its authors.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 2, 6, 13],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["The Lab gives itself as a space, since 2019, for long-term research by Serpentine Arts Technologies and the King's College department into artistic practices working with AI and machine learning. Its stated aim is to surface the 'back-end' knowledge that artistic practice holds about these tools and to overcome 'black-box' narratives. Its funders are listed as King's College London, Serpentine Galleries, the Arts and Humanities Research Council and Google's Artists + Machine Intelligence Grant."]),
  ("What it holds", ["Its feed holds 271 entries, 148 tools and 123 resources, created between 23 June 2020 and 9 May 2024, none of them in 2022. Tools are compiled for their potential to bring new capabilities to artistic workflow, and a tool may be core to the production of the work itself or play a smaller assistive role.",
                     "Each listing carries a column for its maker: tools are headed 'Developed by' and resources 'Author(s)'. Of the 148 tools 144 name a maker, and of the 123 resources 116, as a company or laboratory (OpenAI for Sora, Declare Lab for Mustango), as persons (Seth Forsgren and Hayk Martiros for Riffusion), and for resources as authors with a publisher. The collection is stated to be incomplete, a growing collection of research collected and commissioned by the Lab."]),
  ("How it was made", ["A list of 65 essential tools contributed by the curator and researcher Luba Elliott was a highlight of the launch, and the Lab's timeline records the launch 'with Luba Elliot', so spelled. Tool entries after 2023 are evaluated in their descriptions on five criteria: transparency, creative malleability, technical interoperability, calibre of research and propensity to redistribute."]),
  ("The programme beside it", ["The Lab also runs workshops, public discussions, writing sessions and online tutorials called R&D Labyrinths. One discussion, with Martin Zeilinger on 15 November 2021, addressed the impact of artificial intelligence on human-centered notions of creative agency, authorship and ownership. Its position paper on 'Creative AI' was published through the database in November 2022 (Serpentine lists it as of 2023), and Future Art Ecosystems 4, in March 2024, is the latest item of its timeline. The Lab is listed as ongoing; the feed's latest entry is of 9 May 2024."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The authorship census enters the database as a peripheral comparator whose question is tool discovery, and reads it as meeting the census question at its edge, where the Lab's programme asks about 'creative agency, authorship and ownership' and where the launch credits a named curator. The archive's account of process provenance holds that authorship is a property of the accountable relation between writer, process and text, and that 'Slop is not writing made with AI. Slop is writing without provenance.' Google's AI Overview, asked for the database on 1 November 2026, named no person."])],
 "ko": [[[3, 4, 8]], [[10, 11], [13, 16]], [[7, 15]], [[17, 18, 19]], [[20, 21, 22, 25, 26]]]},

"chatgpt-eating-the-world": {"non_row": "chatgpt-eating-the-world",
 "kernel": "Chat GPT Is Eating the World is a news site and set of trackers on lawsuits against AI companies over copyright and torts, published at chatgptiseatingtheworld.com by an operator its pages do not name, with monthly archives from January 2023. Its unit is a news post on a case or filing, usually with the complaint or order to download.",
 "kernel_ko": [1, 10, 16, 4],
 "sections": [
  ("What it is", ["It tracks the latest developments in AI, by its own account, and its tagline reads \"AI copyright & tort litigation, tracked in real time.\" The site and its newsletter state that it is not affiliated in any way with OpenAI or with the developers of the other AI systems it discusses. Its Substack calls itself \"My personal Substack\" and reports over 2,000 subscribers. It names a sister site, Claude Is Eating the World."]),
  ("What it tracks", ["Its trackers cover AI copyright cases, AI authorship, AI tort lawsuits, AI data center lawsuits, BIPA litigation, and web scraping and AI agents; its menus add a tracker of lawsuits against OpenAI and pages on single cases, Thomson Reuters v. ROSS Intelligence among them. Beside them stand a map of copyright suits against AI companies, a master list of lawsuits last updated on 5 December 2025 and a master chart of claims last updated on 31 March 2026."]),
  ("What it counts", ["Its counts differ by tracker and by date. The latest map story gives 157 copyright suits against AI companies after nine were added; the U.S. map gives 148 suits in the U.S. as of 8 October 2026; the world map gives 130, 100 in the U.S. and 30 elsewhere, as of 5 April 2026, with 143 to stand when it is updated. It states its own limits: its infographic of AI companies sued is \"In need of updating TBA\", its tracker of plaintiffs' firms is \"still in beta\", and one post counts proposed amicus briefs \"By my unofficial count\"."]),
  ("Its own positions", ["Its author takes positions in the litigation it tracks, seeking leave to file an amicus brief in the OpenAI multidistrict litigation and writing opinion pieces. It finds from its own trackers that plaintiffs' firms are driving the AI copyright litigation, and frames the suits as the \"AI Copyright Wars\"."]),
  ("Place in the field", ["The authorship census holds it as an index under governance and a peripheral comparator. Its record keeps suits: authors appear in it as plaintiffs over the use of their works, as textbook authors do in a post on their publishers seeking 85 percent of the Bartz settlement distribution. Authorship itself enters through an AI & Authorship Tracker, an opinion piece on how courts should analyze authorship with AI, and a story on the Copyright Office registering an AI-generated image; the census read none of these.",
                          "The archive reads the doctrine the litigation made as settled by the Supreme Court's denial of certiorari in Thaler v. Perlmutter on 2 March 2026: an AI system cannot be a statutory author of a work claimed to be generated without human authorship. It reads that doctrine as a double enclosure, in which the human composer must choose between honest provenance and full authorial claim."])],
 "ko": [[[2, 3, 10, 13, 15]], [[5, 6]], [[7, 9]], [[11, 12, 10]], [[17, 19, 20], [22, 24]]]},
}
